From 063df6a187d8c098011f9f570371439b35708b74 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: supavise <supavise@users.noreply.invalid>
Date: Sun, 11 Oct 2026 13:17:33 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 6/6] feat(studio): point the AI features at the operator's
 model provider

Studio's AI Assistant and the SQL editor's AI features call OpenAI with
fixed model names, and in platform mode the client never asks the server
whether a key exists: checkOpenAIKey answers { hasKey: true } because
Supabase's own key serves the hosted dashboard. A deployment that runs its
own control plane lets the operator choose the provider (OpenAI, or any API
that speaks its protocol), so the dashboard has to say when none is set.

Server side, read for each request and never sent to the browser:

- SUPAVISE_AI_MODEL replaces the model of the Assistant's chat and
  SUPAVISE_AI_COMPLETION_MODEL the model of the small calls (titles, filters,
  cron, policies, inline completion); the completion model falls back to the
  chat model.
- SUPAVISE_AI_API=chat sends /chat/completions, which every gateway offers,
  instead of /responses. Chat requests leave out store, which is a
  /responses setting.
- SUPAVISE_AI_REASONING_EFFORT is sent when set. A model named through the
  variables gets no upstream effort, because those belong to OpenAI's models.
- OPENAI_API_KEY and OPENAI_BASE_URL are read by @ai-sdk/openai already.

Without the variables every call is upstream's.

The check-api-key route joins the platform allowlist and answers { hasKey,
model }, the model only when the operator chose one; the client now asks it
in platform mode too. The model picker shows that model instead of OpenAI's
three names, and the three messages that told the user to add an
OPENAI_API_KEY point to the server's administrator instead.
